  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Totah Vista's name blends the Navajo word 'Totah,' meaning 'between the waters,' pointing to the angle where the San Juan, Animas, and La Plata rivers meet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/totah_vista_new_mexico",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "At 5,282 feet above sea level, Totah Vista enjoys a high desert setting that shapes its climate.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/totah_vista_new_mexico",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The semi‑arid climate brings hot July highs around 91°F and cold January lows near 18°F, with about 14 inches of snow yearly.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/totah_vista_new_mexico",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "After the Ancestral Puebloans left circa 1300 CE, Athabaskan groups, including the Navajo, settled the area, bringing a semi‑nomadic pastoral lifestyle.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/totah_vista_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In the 1990s and 2000s, Totah Vista grew alongside regional oil and gas expansion, prompting infrastructure upgrades tied to San Juan County's Capital Improvement Plan.",
